            "<p>Now, on the A string, which is the second-fattest string on the bass or guitar, we are \n            going to play an A natural minor scale.  This scale is useful for playing the Blues. \n            We could also play this scale on the E string, or any string, but changing it to the A string starts\n            the scale in a different place than you've just been practicing with, so we want to have you retain the E major\n            sound as the first part of a song, and the A minor sounds as the second part of the same song.  Later we'll play \n            them together in a progression, and you'll follow the changes in sounds and moods and recognized them from your practice,\n            and recognized these changes from other songs you have heard.. \n        </p>",
            "<p>Minor scales are said to be \"darker\", or more melancholic, or less strident than major scales.  \n            In fact many major and minor scales use the same notes but have different relative starting places,\n            so the happy/sad distinction is fairly arbitrary, and many songs, like Blues songs, go back and forth\n            between minor and major fluidly, as a way to change up the mood.  Listen for the changes and\n            think of songs they remind you of while you play. Since it sounds too \"muddy\" to play all the notes \n            at once, scales are a way to tell a story with the notes and weave a journey into the song,\n            while implying chords and \"fabrics\" of note collections that bring the distinctive sounds to different songs.\n        </p>",
            "<p>\n            Put your fingers where the Fingering dots appear on the neck, and play along in time.\n            If you want it slower, click here ...\n        </p>"
          ]
        }
      }
    }
